00:00:30.699 --> 00:00:37.899
Our world today, the one that we have constructed,
feels as if it runs on plastic.

00:00:37.899 --> 00:00:41.850
It is a building block in our bags, our bottles,
clothing, toys…

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:44.937
the list it go on and on.

00:00:44.937 --> 00:00:48.764
Plastic has become so prevalent that it is,
for all intents and purposes,

00:00:48.764 --> 00:00:51.000
impossible to escape.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.751
Even in the microcosmos, plastic is inevitable.

00:00:54.751 --> 00:00:59.760
It persists in long, still threads that
could have come from anything.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:04.364
Maybe it was shed from a piece of clothing,
or it came off a car part.

00:01:04.364 --> 00:01:10.114
Whatever it once was, this shred of microplastic
is one of many pieces just like it turning 

00:01:10.114 --> 00:01:14.272
the microcosmos into a burial ground
for our waste.

00:01:15.772 --> 00:01:23.179
But microbes are crafty, they're capable of making
lives out of the most unusual ingredients—

00:01:23.179 --> 00:01:25.650
including plastic.

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:29.628
So what does that look like, for them 
and also for us?

00:01:30.877 --> 00:01:36.290
Well, first let’s be human about it and focus
on ourselves for a bit, and particularly our

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:37.750
love for plastics.

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:43.465
We currently make around 400 million tons
of plastic every year, but why?

00:01:44.048 --> 00:01:47.530
Well “plastics” covers a wide range of materials.

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:53.100
At a chemical level, they are made of structures
that repeat over and over, creating a pattern

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:59.097
that builds into a material that is moldable
under the right temperatures and pressures.

00:01:59.097 --> 00:02:05.840
And nature is full of materials like this that 
would end up driving our love of plastics, including

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:11.483
natural rubber and plant cellulose, 
even wood is a chemical polymer.

00:02:11.483 --> 00:02:17.402
But nature sets some constraints on how 
much we can take, and precisely what's available.

00:02:17.402 --> 00:02:22.450
And at the turn of the 20th century, the advances
of chemistry and the demands of war meant

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:25.739
that synthetic plastic was primed to take
over—

00:02:25.739 --> 00:02:31.640
first as part of military vehicles and parachutes
and other wartime needs, and then after in

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:34.171
consumer goods like Tupperware.

00:02:34.171 --> 00:02:40.248
They were cheaper and lighter than glass 
and metal, and so versatile.

00:02:40.248 --> 00:02:44.280
But while we’ve lived with the benefits
of these synthetic plastics for more than

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:48.389
a century now, we’re also living with their
harms.

00:02:48.389 --> 00:02:54.376
Plastics don’t just surround us in the objects
we make, they last in these invisible forms,

00:02:54.376 --> 00:02:57.174
smaller than 5 millimeters in length.

00:02:57.174 --> 00:03:02.850
Some might come off our clothes when we wash
them, others might be part of our cosmetics.

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:07.480
They can loft into the air and come back down
with rain, or they can travel through our

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:10.745
waterways, ending up in ponds and oceans.

00:03:10.745 --> 00:03:16.060
James, our master of microscopes, found most
of the samples we’re looking at today floating

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:19.650
in his plankton net while gathering microbe
samples.

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:24.397
And on their own, these small fragments of
plastic seem so inconsequential.

00:03:24.397 --> 00:03:30.422
But in a world like ours, where everything
is connected, nothing is inconsequential.

00:03:30.422 --> 00:03:36.111
Microplastics get eaten by animals, they
break down into chemicals that can be toxic

00:03:36.111 --> 00:03:38.880
and those get leached into the environment.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:41.106
But the consequences do not stop there.

00:03:41.106 --> 00:03:45.882
Animals get eaten by other animals, which
then get eaten by other animals, and so on.

00:03:45.882 --> 00:03:52.350
And with each step up and around the food web,
microplastics and their toxins accumulate.

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:57.569
Scientists are still working to understand
how microplastics affect our ecosystems and

00:03:57.569 --> 00:03:58.771
our bodies.

00:03:58.771 --> 00:04:02.691
But one of the challenges with microplastics
is just how durable they are.

00:04:02.691 --> 00:04:07.624
Those long chains of atoms, once assembled,
tend to stay that way.

00:04:08.409 --> 00:04:14.340
One way to curb the amount of microplastics
that end up in our world and in us is to stop

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:19.932
making as much new plastic and instead, you 
know, recycle what we’ve already made.

00:04:19.932 --> 00:04:24.790
Unfortunately, only a small fraction of the
massive amount of plastic we produce each

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:26.815
year gets recycled.

00:04:26.815 --> 00:04:31.320
Some of the challenges are in our infrastructure,
like figuring out how to sort different types

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:36.404
of plastics so they can be properly recycled
without mixing them up with each other.

00:04:36.404 --> 00:04:41.139
Some of it is technological, in finding ways
to break down the most challenging plastics,

00:04:41.139 --> 00:04:46.759
or developing tools to ensure recycled plastic
is just as good as the original.

00:04:46.759 --> 00:04:52.750
And that has led scientists to turn to the
microcosmos for possible answers, with one

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:54.620
particular question in mind:

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:57.809
can microbes eat plastic?

00:04:58.642 --> 00:05:03.010
Now there are many ways to investigate 
that question, and for Maria Pinto—

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:05.610
a scientist who has sent us samples before—

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:12.940
it’s taken her out onto the ocean as she
looks to see how bacteria gather on plastics.

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:15.183
The samples come from all over.

00:05:15.183 --> 00:05:20.645
Some are from the Atlantic Ocean, some from
the Pacific Ocean, some from the Adriatic Sea.

00:05:20.645 --> 00:05:26.069
The ubiquity of microplastics in her samples
feels similar to the way we find microbes

00:05:26.069 --> 00:05:30.922
everywhere, but without the wonder and
awe that microbes inspire.

00:05:30.922 --> 00:05:36.780
Now microbes are everywhere because they find
a way to endure, adapt, and spread.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:39.825
Their ubiquity is a marker of their ingenuity.

00:05:39.825 --> 00:05:46.120
Microplastics, on the other hand, are a product
of our ingenuity, but also of our carelessness

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:47.805
with our creations.

00:05:47.805 --> 00:05:53.290
It also fits that these two pervasive
entities would find each other.

00:05:53.290 --> 00:06:00.160
Maria has seen the bacteria gathering on microplastics,
their cellular bodies producing enzymes that

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:07.500
might even be able to break down the plastics
into their individual units for the bacteria to use.

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:10.699
Scientists are looking in plenty of other
places too.

00:06:10.699 --> 00:06:11.789
Greek beaches.

00:06:11.789 --> 00:06:14.720
Mud near a Japanese plastic recycling factory.

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:17.265
Hot springs in Yellowstone National Park.

00:06:17.265 --> 00:06:23.060
These diverse iterations of the microcosmos
and the plastic-eating bacteria in them teach

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:29.650
us more about the enzymes we might be able
to use in the future to recycle plastic.

00:06:29.650 --> 00:06:32.402
These enzymes come with their own challenges.

00:06:32.402 --> 00:06:36.579
Some work in a narrow temperature range, others
are just kind of slow.

00:06:36.579 --> 00:06:40.429
But the more scientists learn about 
how these enzymes work, 

00:06:40.429 --> 00:06:42.476
the more they’re able to improve them—

00:06:42.476 --> 00:06:47.781
sometimes even combining different enzymes
to make one big super plastic-digesting enzyme.

00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:54.190
This is all necessary research, urgent
against the backdrop of every other way our

00:06:54.190 --> 00:06:57.479
planet has been affected by human behavior.

00:06:57.479 --> 00:07:03.750
We are still only just beginning to understand
how widespread this evolution around plastics

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:11.259
is, and how organisms have managed to develop
mechanisms around seemingly unnatural materials

00:07:11.259 --> 00:07:14.010
that we’ve overwhelmed nature with.

00:07:14.010 --> 00:07:19.181
In 2020, we knew of about 90 microbes 
capable of digesting plastic,

00:07:19.181 --> 00:07:23.810
though scientists are still trying to understand
whether all those microbes consume the parts

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:25.353
we want them to.

00:07:25.353 --> 00:07:31.169
And more research searching for signs
of plastic-digesting enzymes found a correlation

00:07:31.169 --> 00:07:37.730
between the abundance of those enzymes and
the extent of plastic pollution in a given area.

00:07:37.730 --> 00:07:43.771
Perhaps if we understand more about how microbes
are adapting to this pollution, we’ll understand

00:07:43.771 --> 00:07:46.140
better how we can use them.

00:07:46.926 --> 00:07:51.596
But what does it mean to rely 
on our oldest, smallest creatures

00:07:51.596 --> 00:07:55.000
to fix our newest, biggest problems?

00:07:55.009 --> 00:07:59.548
A bacterium can eat away at plastic, but what
comes after?

00:07:59.548 --> 00:08:03.150
When you think of the scale of what bacteria
have done in the past, it seems possible for

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:05.010
them to fix things for us.

00:08:05.010 --> 00:08:10.787
They’ve reshaped atmospheres and constructed
ecosystems, what’s a little plastic to them?

00:08:10.787 --> 00:08:14.550
But for all bacteria can do, they can’t
run our business.

00:08:14.550 --> 00:08:18.934
Nor can they command our infrastructure or
curb our demands.

00:08:18.934 --> 00:08:21.796
Bacteria are not going to be our saviors, 

00:08:21.796 --> 00:08:26.210
they are simply our neighbors, 
along with everything else on earth.

00:08:26.210 --> 00:08:31.389
So to make the best use of them then requires
more than just understanding how they work.

00:08:31.389 --> 00:08:35.219
It requires understanding ourselves, and how
we work.

00:08:35.219 --> 00:08:39.074
Anything else, and it may not matter
what we find.

00:08:43.893 --> 00:08:49.344
